a7d967fb-ec7f-4d6e-b6f8-d5a0f281be6a	All Advanced Placement (AP) Computer Science is Not Created Equal: A Comparison of AP Computer Science A and Computer Science Principles	This article compares the two most prominent courses of Advanced Placement (AP) computer science study offered throughout 9-12 grades in the U.S. The structure, guidelines, components, and exam formats of the traditional AP Computer Science A course and the relatively newer AP Computer Science Principles course were compared to examine differences in content and emphases. A depth-of-learning analysis was conducted employing Bloom’s Revised Taxonomy to examine potential differences in rigor and challenge represented by the two options, particularly as it relates to acquiring computer programming proficiency. Analyses suggest structural differences in both course content and end-of-course exam components likely result in less depth and rigor in the new Computer Science Principles course as compared to the Computer Science A course. A lower minimum standard for learning programming skills in the Computer Science Principles course was observed, making it a less viable option for students looking to acquire skills transferable to future computer science study or employment. The potential implications for students choosing the new course over the traditional offering, as well as for schools opting for the new course as its sole or primary offering are discussed.	{"AP Computer Science Principles","course comparison","course rigor","programming skills","Bloom's revised taxonomy","depth of learning","test format","high school computer science education","course content"}	https://doi.org/10.26716/jcsi.2019.02.1.2	\N	https://doi.org/10.26716/jcsi.2019.02.1.2	2019	2019-02-21	Journal of Computer Science Integration	\N	OpenAlex	https://openalex.org/W2917348181	2026-11-20 20:04:07.893131+00	2026-11-20 20:04:07.893131+00
a838027b-ae8c-4ba0-a779-eebb333866ff	Girls, computer science, and games	An innovative week long mini-course for girls has successfully used video game development as the main motivation by teaching related computer science topics at the same time. Students split their time between creating their own game in the lab and learning about game design, usability, graphics, and artificial intelligence. They became more comfortable with the subject and say they are more likely to pursue computer science in high school because of this course.	{"girls in computer science","video game development","game design","computer science education",minicourse,"artificial intelligence",usability,graphics,"high school students","gender diversity in computing"}	https://doi.org/10.1145/1473195.1473233	\N	https://doi.org/10.1145/1473195.1473233	2008	2008-11-30	ACM SIGCSE Bulletin	Association for Computing Machinery	OpenAlex	https://openalex.org/W2054673081	2026-11-20 20:04:07.893131+00	2026-11-20 20:04:07.893131+00
a875088b-762a-4f17-91b3-9b634c85d644	Predicting performance in an introductory computer science course	A group of 269 first-semester freshmen was used to predict both performance in an introductory computer science course and first-semester college grade point average by using information regarding the students' programs and performance in high school along with American College Testing Program (ACT) test scores.	{"introductory computer science","student performance prediction","college grade point average","ACT scores","secondary school performance"}	https://doi.org/10.1145/3166.3167	\N	https://doi.org/10.1145/3166.3167	1985	1985-03-01	Communications of the ACM	Association for Computing Machinery	OpenAlex	https://openalex.org/W2003909323	2026-11-20 20:04:07.893131+00	2026-11-20 20:04:07.893131+00
a87675bf-78aa-4bd6-a47f-6ea8f011f2fb	Laboratory-style teaching of computer science	Laboratory-style teaching of computer science is discussed.The paper describes an approach, used for some years by the nuthors, that wus developed after studying the methods used in the traditional laboratory sciences; its use helps to establish the equivalence for funding purposes of computer science with other laboratory sciences.Examples are given, and techniques that have been found useftd are described.The factors to be considered when setting tests for grading are examined in detail.	{"computer science education",grading,"assessment methods","teaching methods"}	https://doi.org/10.1145/319059.323449	\N	https://doi.org/10.1145/319059.323449	1990	1990-02-01	ACM SIGCSE Bulletin	Association for Computing Machinery	OpenAlex	https://openalex.org/W2047287381	2026-11-20 20:04:07.893131+00	2026-11-20 20:04:07.893131+00
a8e06c09-5e3b-4c93-8514-abc0caa8302e	Computer service and computer science	Dennis Gilles retired on 30 September 1990, after 33 years at Glasgow University, as its first director of the Computing Laboratory and first Professor of Computer Science. A seminar to mark the occasion of Professor Gilles’s retirement was held on 26 September. The text that follows is based on a talk given at that seminar but has been expanded to incorporate some recent published ideas.	{"computer science",retirement,"history of computing"}	https://doi.org/10.1093/combul/34.1.20	\N	https://doi.org/10.1093/combul/34.1.20	1992	1992-02-01	ITNOW	Oxford University Press	OpenAlex	https://openalex.org/W2292915937	2026-11-20 20:04:07.893131+00	2026-11-20 20:04:07.893131+00
